  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The empirical evidence is drawn from a single case study of a specific tourism and leisure living lab in Rotterdam, which may limit direct generalizability to other urban planning contexts.",
      "what": "This paper critiques neoliberal placemaking practices through a case study of Rotterdam's Urban Leisure and Tourism Living Lab. It analyzes how top-down regeneration initiatives commodify urban spaces, leading to gentrification and displacement, and contrasts these with citizen perspectives from local urban activation projects.",
      "why": "It demonstrates how well-intentioned, participatory-labeled planning initiatives can actively marginalize vulnerable communities, offering a framework for bottom-up, community-focused placemaking that aligns with the paradigm of New Urban Tourism."
